{#-
  The list of pages we want ranked — not a list of pages that exist. Search
  Console reads this as our own statement of what matters, so anything in here
  that is thin, transactional or half-rendered costs more than it earns.

  /help/ was deliberately out until 24 Aug 2026, on #135's reasoning: the
  served HTML was a shell whose entire body copy was the word "Loading…".
  #136 prerendered it and #166 gave each guide its own URL. Since studio #1201
  (7 Oct 2026) the section is short articles, one question each, and each one
  is listed below, DERIVED from src/_data/help.js: an article the studio adds
  is in the sitemap at the next build with nobody editing this file, and one
  it retires leaves it the same way. /help/ itself stays in the list as the
  hub somebody typing "prospektor help" wants.

  Deliberately out, each for its own reason:
    /checkout/       a form, not an answer to any search. Left crawlable and
                     linked from the homepage CTA, so nothing is lost; if
                     Search Console ever shows it earning buy-intent
                     impressions, #137 can put it back with evidence rather
                     than on a hunch. (Decided #135, 24 Aug 2026.)
    /checkout/done/  noindex — what a buyer reads seconds after paying.
    /404.html        noindex.
    /app/            301s to /; whether that page has a future is W2's
                     question and a sitemap should not pre-empt it.

  /integrations/close/ joined on 10 Sep 2026 (#620). It is the page Close's
  integration directory points at, and it is also this site's own best answer
  to somebody typing "prospektor close" — which is exactly what this file is
  for. Its language twins arrive the same derived way every other page's do.

  The seven pages after the Close page joined on 29 Sep 2026 (#841): one
  landing page per integration, in the Close page's shape, each the answer
  to "prospektor <vendor>".

  /integrations/ joined on 28 Sep 2026 (#838): the directory the Close page
  sits under, every app the studio's own Connect your apps grid holds, one
  card each, the page "prospektor integrations" wants.

  /demo/ joined on 28 Sep 2026 (#767). It is the product's own screens with
  one sentence each, the page somebody typing "prospektor demo" wants, and
  its ten sentences are real copy rather than a shell. Its language twins
  arrive the derived way every other page's do.

  /contact/ joined on 31 Aug 2026 (#456). It is in for the same reason
  /pricing/ is: somebody types "prospektor contact", it is this site's own best
  answer to that, and a reachable contact page is a trust signal on a site
  asking $999/month of a stranger. /contact/thanks/ is OUT and noindex — the
  /checkout/done/ case exactly: thin, transactional, and meaningless to anyone
  who lands on it cold.

  /who-to-pitch/, /what-to-send/ and /pricing/ joined on 24 Aug 2026 (#153),
  when they stopped being anchors on the homepage and became pages. They are
  exactly what this file is for: each answers a question somebody types, each
  is the site's own best page on that question, and none of them is thin — the
  reason /checkout/ is still out is that it answers nothing.

  **The other languages (#114) are derived the same way the guides are.** Each
  page listed above is asked which languages the build wrote it in — the
  `alternates` filter, the same one the layout's hreflang links come from — and
  every twin is listed beside it. So `/es/pricing/` is in the sitemap because
  `src/_data/strings/es.json` exists and pricing.njk paginates over the
  languages, and nobody lists it here; a language retired by deleting its
  catalogue leaves the sitemap the same way. A page that is translated on its
  own URL is a page worth ranking on that URL, for the same reason its English
  twin is — and `hreflang` on the page tells Google which is which.

  **The help articles in another language (#535) follow the same rule twice
  over.** An edition exists only where the studio holds at least one article in
  that language (src/_data/help.js), and within it only an article the studio
  served IN that language is listed — one it fell back to English on is written, for
  the reader who followed the hub, but is `noindex` and left out here: its
  English twin is the page to rank, and a sitemap must not ask for a URL the
  page itself declines.

  <lastmod>, added by #144 on exactly the terms #135 set. The static pages
  still carry none, because a build-time stamp would claim every page changed
  on every deploy and Google ignores a date it cannot trust. The articles carry
  one because theirs is real: it is the article's own published/updated date
  from its frontmatter, it moves only when the article does, and it is the
  thing a crawler should come back for.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for p in pages %}
{%- for a in p | alternates %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ a.url }}</loc></url>
{%- endfor %}
{%- endfor %}
{#- /partners/ (#885) is listed exactly when the programme is on: dark, the
    page is one line and noindex (src/partners.11tydata.js), and a sitemap
    must not ask for a URL the page itself declines. -#}
{%- if rewardful.on %}
{%- for a in '/partners/' | alternates %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ a.url }}</loc></url>
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- for e in help.editions %}{%- for a in e.articles %}{%- if a.language == e.code %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ e.prefix }}/help/{{ a.slug }}/</loc></url>
{%- endif %}{%- endfor %}{%- endfor %}
{%- for a in collections.resources | related('', 99) %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ a.url }}</loc><lastmod>{{ (a.data.updated or a.data.date) | isoDate }}</lastmod></url>
{%- endfor %}
{#- The lessons of /learn/ (#742), derived from the snapshot the pages are
    drawn from, the way the articles are from their collection: nine pages
    that each answer one question a new customer types, in the mail's own
    words, and none of them thin. No <lastmod>: the date the snapshot was
    taken is the date the words were copied, not the date they changed. -#}
{%- for l in lessons.lessons %}
  <url><loc>{{ site.url }}{{ l.url }}</loc></url>
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
